 "notes": [
  "COURT \u25b8 EVENTS (client/scenes/court/events_view.gd), from events.png (art/slices/events.json). Every rect is the painting's; the foot is pinned to the screen's foot, so on a tall phone the panels stand where they were painted and the leaves stay at the bottom.",
  "hero: the festival running, else the next announced, else the last to close (dimmed), else none. hero_scene is the festival's own scene (events/scene_<theme>_hero, event_themes.png drawn down) under the frame, whose window is cleared. hero_title: the name in the title plate (gold rim x 185..903, y 661..714; inside y 666..710), Cinzel 28 in the store's section gold, left; hero_bonus: what it gives this lord, Garamond 24, right, on the same plate. hero_timer: the time in the timer plate (steel x 255..494, y 735..780), Garamond 26 as the Court's lines. view: VIEW where it was painted (x 645..897, y 723..787); view_badge/count: the Court's count disc on VIEW's corner while something waits to be claimed.",
  "card_*: the two cards (left steel x 161..529, right 537..928, y 815..1169): the festival's scene (events/scene_<theme>_left|right) under the frame; name in the title plate (x 180..511 and 556..909, y 1046..1097), Cinzel 24 gold; its time in the timer plate (x 255..459 and 646..843, y 1110..1149); card_badge_*: the Court's disc (court/badge) where the painting's red disc was (centres x 493 and 894.5, y 842.5), only while the card has something to claim.",
  "hours_*: ROYAL HOURS: the hour's event (its icon, hourly/<id>, drawn down into the name plate's left; its name) in the name plate (x 296..536, y 1236..1285), its time in the timer plate (x 591..727, y 1242..1282), the next hour's in NEXT's plate (x 759..899, y 1239..1282). A tap opens the hours' odds.",
  "up_row: UPCOMING's rows (court/event_row, the painting's own row with its calendar lifted) at the painting's pitch 69 (y 1357, 1426, 1495): the calendar for what is announced, the hourglass for an operator's event running now. Their words where the Wave 2 events panel set them.",
  "The painting's small red badge on each card is not cut: a card is a festival announced or past, and neither ever has anything to claim -- what a festival still owes a lord comes by letter when it closes."
 ],
